    "description": "Life expectancy summarizes the mortality conditions facing a population at a particular time. Iran’s recent international estimates show sustained improvement before a sharp pandemic-period interruption and a subsequent recovery, while national averages leave important differences in health and survival unresolved.",
    "tags": ["Life expectancy", "Mortality", "Population health", "Healthy aging", "Demographic estimates"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T03:57:54Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T03:57:54Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Life Expectancy in Iran: Trends, Meaning and Limitations",
      "description": "Explore Iran’s annual life-expectancy estimates, the recent interruption and recovery, and the distinction between longevity and healthy life."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "meaning-of-life-expectancy",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A measure of mortality conditions",
        "content": "Life expectancy at birth is the number of years a newborn would live if the mortality patterns prevailing at birth continued throughout life. It is a conditional statistical summary, rather than a prediction of the lifespan of a particular child. Future medical advances, epidemics, injuries, and other changes can alter the mortality conditions that an actual generation experiences. [s2]\n\nThe measure incorporates mortality across the life course. It should therefore be read alongside age-specific survival and other health indicators, rather than treated as a direct description of older people’s remaining years. A national figure also compresses a varied population into one summary. The dataset here covers both sexes together and does not establish identical survival conditions for men and women or across Iran’s provinces. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "recent-pattern",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Improvement, interruption, and recovery",
        "content": "The World Development Indicators series reproduced by FRED estimates life expectancy at 75.039 years in 2012 and 76.855 years in 2019. The upward movement was interrupted: the estimate fell to 74.141 in 2020 and 73.752 in 2021. It subsequently rose to 76.799 in 2022 and 77.854 in 2024. These figures describe the inspected release, whose historical values may differ from earlier publications. [s1]\n\nThe timing identifies a pandemic-period interruption, but this all-cause series cannot on its own allocate the decline among direct infection deaths, disrupted care, or other influences. Such attribution requires cause-specific research. It would also be misleading to splice values from another institution into this line merely because that institution reports a similar indicator. [s1][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "life-expectancy-chart",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "line",
          "title": "Iran: estimated life expectancy at birth, 2012–2026",
          "dataset_id": "iran-life-expectancy",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["life-expectancy-total"],
          "x_axis_label": "Gregorian reporting year",
          "y_axis_label": "Years of life",
          "y_axis_min": 0,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "The estimate improves through 2019, falls in 2020 and 2021, and exceeds its 2019 level by 2023. The inspected series ends in 2024."
        },
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "health-and-longevity",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Longer life and healthy life are different questions",
        "content": "The World Health Organization distinguishes life expectancy from healthy life expectancy, or HALE. The latter accounts for time lived in less than full health because of disease or injury. A population can consequently gain years of life without gaining the same amount of time in full health. The two measures answer related but different questions, and should retain their separate definitions. [s3]\n\nWHO also explains why modeled estimates can differ from national life tables: incomplete mortality information requires statistical estimation. For Iran, it cautions that death-registration completeness and cause assignment affect the interpretation of estimated causes of death. Those warnings are especially relevant when using mortality statistics to judge the performance of a particular programme. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-responsibly",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the trend can establish",
        "content": "The annual line provides a consistent description of the direction and timing of change within one published series. It does not measure the experience of every resident or explain every cause of improvement. Its value is strongest when the definition, source, and reference year remain visible. [s1][s2]\n\nFor understanding Iran, the distinction between survival and health is particularly useful: the life-expectancy series describes mortality conditions, while healthy-life and age-specific measures are needed to examine the quality and distribution of longer lives. Neither national longevity nor its recovery should be read as an individual medical prognosis. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
