  {
    "schema_version": "1.0",
    "page_id": "topic-10-1-4",
    "page_path": "/explore/society/demography/population-aging/",
    "name": "Population Aging",
    "level": 3,
    "parent_path": "/explore/society/demography/",
    "entity_type": "concept",
    "address": null,
    "phone": null,
    "email": null,
    "website": null,
    "logo": null,
    "contact_source_ids": [],
    "description": "Population aging describes a shift toward older ages within the population, rather than the aging of individuals alone. Iran’s rising older-age share reflects its fertility and mortality transition and raises questions about accessible communities, income security, care, and participation in later life.",
    "tags": ["Population aging", "Older people", "Age structure", "Social care", "Demographic transition", "Accessibility"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-28T03:57:54Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-28T03:57:54Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Population Aging in Iran: Older Age, Care and Social Change",
      "description": "Explore Iran’s rising older-age share, the difference between age thresholds, and the social questions surrounding longer lives."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "defining-aging",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A change in the population’s composition",
        "content": "Population aging means that older age groups account for a larger share of residents. In Iran, UNFPA connects this transition to declining fertility and increased life expectancy. Smaller younger generations and longer survival change the balance between ages, although the resulting population remains diverse in health, resources, and personal circumstances. [s3][s4]\n\nThe age threshold must always be stated. The annual indicator used here measures residents aged 65 and above as a percentage of the total population. UNFPA’s discussion of older people also uses a 60-year threshold. These categories overlap but are not interchangeable; a percentage for one cannot be substituted for the other. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "annual-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A steadily rising older-age share",
        "content": "The inspected World Development Indicators series estimates that residents aged 65 and above represented approximately 5.43 percent of Iran’s population in 2012 and 8.58 percent in 2025. Every intervening annual estimate in this window is higher than the preceding one. The underlying dataset retains the source’s numerical precision; these rounded percentages are only for readable prose. [s1]\n\nWorld Bank metadata defines the denominator as the resident population, regardless of citizenship or legal status, and identifies the values as midyear estimates based on United Nations age distributions. This is a population share at a reference point, not an annual count of people entering retirement. The metadata also warns that annualized age estimates may not reproduce every short-term event. [s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "older-share-chart",
        "type": "chart",
        "content": {
          "type": "line",
          "title": "Iran: estimated population aged 65 and above, 2012–2026",
          "dataset_id": "iran-older-population-share",
          "x_field": "period_label",
          "series_ids": ["population-65-plus-share"],
          "x_axis_label": "Gregorian reporting year; midyear estimate",
          "y_axis_label": "Percent of all residents, 0–100 scale",
          "y_axis_min": 0,
          "show_legend": false,
          "connect_nulls": false,
          "interpretation": "The published older-age share increases throughout 2012–2025. No value is supplied for 2026, and this series should not be combined with statistics using a 60-plus threshold."
        },
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "diverse-later-lives",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Later life is not one uniform experience",
        "content": "The UNFPA and HelpAge policy case study emphasizes that older people have different needs and that no single ministry can provide every relevant service. It identifies gender inequality and socioeconomic insecurity as concerns, while presenting collaboration among public bodies, municipalities, civil society, older people, and families as part of the policy framework. [s4]\n\nThat approach broadens the subject beyond medical treatment. An accessible neighborhood, reliable support, and opportunities for participation concern daily life as well as clinical care. UNFPA describes its work in Iran in terms of an age-friendly and disability-friendly environment and investment across the life course. These are stated programme aims, rather than evidence that every locality already provides such conditions. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3", "s4"]
      },
      {
        "id": "policy-and-evidence",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Policy commitments and demonstrated outcomes",
        "content": "UNFPA reports that the National Document of Ageing is under implementation and identifies the State Welfare Organization’s elderly-policy secretariat and the Ministry of Health and Medical Education as partners. This establishes the agency’s account of the institutional framework. It does not independently demonstrate service quality, universal coverage, or adequate income protection. [s3]\n\nThe case study presents the national document as a framework for coordinated action. Its practical implication is that aging policy involves several institutions and levels of society. Assessing results requires evidence about what older residents actually receive, including differences between communities and between people with different support needs. [s4]\n\nIran’s rising older-age share is therefore a clear demographic finding. The adequacy of the social response is a separate empirical question, and neither an age threshold nor a population chart can answer it alone. [s1][s4]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s3", "s4"]
      }
